A||||43||count_iblis||heavy fiero||||1||
Z||000000||count_iblis||10-05-2000||08:08 AM||||Why is the fiero so heavy?  I mean just looking at it and buy the way is sounds it shouldnt be heavy at all weighing in about 2700 lbs.  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||loafer87gt||10-05-2000||11:43 AM||||From what I've read, the space frame isn't all that weight efficient of a design. The is a lot of structural reinforcing going on under the fibreglass bodywork. I wish they would have been able to manufacture the aluminum spaceframe that ALCAN had been working on. It was supposed to cut the weight of the spaceframe almost by half. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Black88GT||10-05-2000||01:30 PM||||loafer, it cut the weight of the space frame 33% according to the gary witzenburg book.<BR>They made a couple but they were never mass produced.  If they would have only made that '89 fiero....<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
Z||000003||ChadMan||10-05-2000||01:39 PM||||You know, compared to modern similarly-sized sports cars, the Fiero really isn't that heavy.  The Boxster, Audi TT, Mercedes CLK and BMW Z3 all outweigh the Fiero by quite a bit.<P>The iron block and heads add weight, too.  I would think a Quad 4-equipped Fiero would weigh around 2350-2500 lbs, depending on what options it had.||||reg||1
Z||000004||loafer87gt||10-05-2000||02:06 PM||||All the cars mentioned above are also convertibles, which commonly adds 200+ pnds to the overall weight of the car due to the extra crossbracing needed.||||reg||1
Z||000005||count_iblis||10-05-2000||02:17 PM||||im just saying like compare to the miata,<P>mx3, del sol, and the new zx2, dont they <P>all weigh less than the two seater fiero?  i <P>think that the new zx2 wieghs in at only <P>2300-2400 lbs.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||count_iblis||10-05-2000||02:20 PM||||I dont keep facts well like i should, but<P>how much hp does the V6 fiero gt have?  <P>Alot of people are talking about the quad <P>4.  What is the hp difference in the two?  <P>How much tourqe are you losing?  wouldnt it <P>be two much trouble replacing the v6 with <P>the quad 4?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Black88GT||10-05-2000||03:06 PM||||The fiero does weigh a lot.  Motor Trend was highly critical of that fact when reviewing the '88 GT.  I have heard you can lose about 100 lbs. by taking out "un-necessary" things, but mainly hardcore racers (or wannabe hardcore racers) are the only ones that do that.<P>The stock fiero 2.8 puts out about 140 horses.  i.e. the reason why its so slow in stock form.  <BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
Z||000008||count_iblis||10-05-2000||03:11 PM||||140hp  ouch!  so that is close to about what 132 hp at the wheels?  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||lou_dias||10-05-2000||03:24 PM||||more like 110 at the wheels...from dynos I've seen...<BR>My 3.4 makes 146.3 at the wheels||||reg||1
Z||000010||mrfixit58||10-05-2000||03:53 PM||||Another weight-y object is the rear spindles. I was amazed at just how heavy they were. If thay would have cast them in aluminum like the Corvette, you could probably shave 40# off the rear.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||13
Z||000011||hugh||10-05-2000||06:08 PM||||My 88 GT weighs 3400# with me in it and a full tank of gas(now that's heavy).It weighs 1200 front and 2200 rear.That's with aluminum heads on a 350 chevy mated to a 425a transaxle.||||reg||1
Z||000012||ur2biteme||10-05-2000||09:39 PM||||from everything i've read, the fiero was built with safety in mind.  if memory serves me correctly, it was rated as the safest car in america in many categories all 5 years of production.  <P>this car has a full rollcage.  i'm sure that makes up the weight difference for any crossbracing on any of these convertables mentioned (kinda like taking the roof reinforcement and putting it underneath instead).  the reason for the addition from what i've read is that, at least at head level, they added this reinforcement due to the fact that the fiero sat so much lower to the ground as compared with all the other cars at the time, or now for that matter (excursion? that's not an S.U.V., it's a B-U-S!).<P>all i know is that i've heard a lot more stories of other cars mangaling their driver in an accident.  then i hear about the fiero.  someone gets into some wild accident (sometimes driver stupidity, sometimes the other driver's stupidity, sometimes it's unavoidable in either circumstance, other than staying at home) both cars are completely demolished, and the person in the fiero walked away.  <P>i went into purchasing my first fiero because my mom commmented on how fun her '88 coupe was before it got repo-ed, but didn't expect to find such a well-rounded vehicle from any car's standpoint, much less a sports car.  the one thing i didn't expect when i bought mine was peace of mind that this car will probably fare better than most in an accident. <P>2700# isn't that heavy.  some examples of heavier off the top of my head:  viper at around 4000#, Mustang (i owned a 94) 3500#, if memory serves me correctly the f bodies run about 3800#, and not much is mentioned about their weight.  or recall problems (low speed impacts on mustang hoods pre-96 caused the skin to seperate at freeway speed, causing the sheet metal to cover the windshield) and i'm sure there are plenty to go around for those as well.<P>this is not intended as a flame, because believe me, i still want my mustang back, i like the viper, and, the f bodies, i'm not gonna talk bad about them, i just don't like their styling.  <P>also too, the fiero was light for it's time too, 12-17 years ago, and it's still comparable to modern equivalents to it's class.  ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Xtreme Fiero's||10-05-2000||10:06 PM|||| about a year ago a month after i got my second fiero, I spun my 84 SE going 90mph on a windy ass back road I did 3 180° spins in oposite directons and slamed into a concrete wall on the side of a small bridge (about 1' high) both me & co-pilot where ok and so was the car (for a 30mph impact with a wall  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ) i bent the rear left A-arm and put a dent in the floor of the trunk. I say the fiero is damn safe  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><BR>PS The crash was not driver error the back left cradle bold FELL OUT!!! it had been repaced for MD inspection a week before as a shop!||||reg||1
Z||000014||count_iblis||10-06-2000||07:29 AM||||I know that the fiero is safe, i saw a totaled fiero before and the cockpit was left unharmed.  so kudos to the fiero||||reg||1
Z||000015||deceler8||10-06-2000||11:18 AM||||If you think it's heavy now...imagine a 2000 Fiero that would have to have driver, passenger and probably side air bags, not to mention all the other safety stuff mandated today...<P>||||reg||1
Z||000016||frontal lobe||10-06-2000||07:24 PM||||2700 pounds is heavy for the size of the car, maybe, but compared to what else you can buy now with any decent output it's really pretty light.  Yes, only 140hp but 170 torque.  When you get old like me you really don't want to put your foot into it ALL the time, so the torque is much appreciated.<P>I have a Miata, too, and it is nice that it weighs only about 2,300 pounds.  Very fling-able.  But it has NO guts.  I have to really whomp on it for it to do anything, which is fun for a while but gets tiring around town.<P>So give me the 2700 pounds with 170 torque and I'll take that anytime.<P>Check out the weight of available cars with that kind of torque for sale now.  Likely to weigh 3300 minimum.<P>That said, could GM have done a better job of keeping the weight down--yes.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Ed||10-06-2000||09:01 PM||||Mr. Lobe:<P>Your comments on the Miata struck a cord because one of our long-term Sacramento, CA, Fiero club members/owners (of two, no less) sold both of them and bought a turbo 300ZX and a Miata.  He was really talking up the Miata, but I've test driven the Miata and would have to agree with you. <P>Sometimes a little more weight makes a car better for a different purpose: A "GT" versus a "sports car"  and I've had both.  To each his own, although doesn't everyone always wish for a better power to weight ratio? <P>I was looking through a Porsche magazine and even they are always changing this or that in the elusive struggle for perfection in automobiles. ||||reg||1
Z||000018||mrfiero||10-06-2000||10:50 PM||||Here's proof of the Fieros durability in an accident.  This is/was an '88 GT.  I bought it a year ago as a parts car.  Evidently the owner decided to go really fast on a rain slicked road.....he hydroplaned, spun out and hit a tree or a pole.  They (there were 2 people inside) had to be extricated from the car....that's why the roof is gone....but both survived.  The passengers legs were crushed, but nothing life-threatening.<P>I couldn't seem to load PIP, so I uploaded it to honesty.com.  Here is the link:<P> <A HREF="http://images.honesty.com/imagedata/5/2/1/15809521.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://images.honesty.com/imagedata/5/2/1/15809521.jpg</A> <P><BR>BTW.....it's a huge picture, so you'll be able to see better details of the damaged area. <BR>  <p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 10-06-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000019||88formula||10-06-2000||11:25 PM||||The Honda S2000 weighs 2700 lbs. and is a great handling sports car!||||reg||1
Z||000020||frontal lobe||10-06-2000||11:49 PM||||88formula, no doubt about the Honda s2000 being an excellent handling car.  This is my point, though.  The car weighs 2700 pounds, and is one of the lightest cars you can buy now (OK, metros excluded, I mean sporty).<P>Ed, I can't blame the former Fiero owner for wanting a Nissan 300zx turbo.  I'd love that kind of power.  But again, look at the weight it carries.  I'm not sure what it is, but I think at least 3300.  That's still a good power to weight ratio, but I was looking for a lighter weight (and lighter cost!!) option.<P>There is no "perfect" car, because people have individual tastes and preferences.  I can appreciate a wide array.  To me, the Fiero strikes a nice balance in v6 form.  (V8 form seems pretty appealing, too! but I've never driven one).||||reg||1
Z||000021||fieroparts.com||10-07-2000||01:03 AM||||mrfiero<P><BR>holy s-it batman<P>||||reg||1
Z||000022||Cheever2||10-07-2000||09:05 PM||||I lost control in the wet, doing 60, slammed the concrete wall on I-610 right in front of the Astrodome.  I saw that wall coming and KNEW I was gonna die.  Hit hard, spun around, did it again 2 more times before stopping.  Walked away from it.  Looked it over, then DROVE away from it.  All is still perfect except the body and the frame bent in at the right-front corner.  This was my 88 GT.<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000023||Philphine||10-08-2000||04:31 PM||||a lot of the cars mentioned have, if not all aluminum engines, major aluminum parts like heads, plus more hp. having only had 4 cyl. fieros i think the power/ weight ratio gives even more of a heavy feeling bcause of how sluggish it is. if you considered all the swaps we talk about with the exception of v8s' and probaly 3.8 v6's, the power gain with little added weight would negate alot of other lighter cars weight advantage. i guess thats pretty obvious.<P>when i factor in weight i'm thinkng the best power plant would be the 3.4tdc engine. i read it's a little lighter than an all iron 2.8 and with a 4:10 tranny it might make up for the low end torque lost, but thats all just guessing. <P>a quad 4 would probaly be best weightwise but i have a hard time with "wanting" another 4 cyl. engine.<P>my dream engine has become an aluminum small block. the weight advantage of a north star without the complication. <P>------------------<BR>Phil T<P>'87 coupe (feral)<BR>'84 indy<BR>'84 coupe (cheap thrill)||||reg||1
Z||000024||Black88GT||10-09-2000||05:45 PM||||Oops, looks like I made a mistake.<P>According to the G. Witzenburg Fiero book...<P>"Improvements seemed to be extending into the future as well.  One Fiero project involved an expiramental aluminum spaceframe devised by Alcan Automotive Structures of Canada.  Alcan, with cooperation from Entech and GM's CPC Group, had the aluminum spaceframe pressed in the same dies and in the same gauges as the Fiero's conventional steel structure.  Two Alcan Fieros were built in 1986, both using special bonding adhesives that completely eliminated welding.  The aluminem spaceframes weighed 68% less than their steel counterparts.  They also boosted torsional stiffness by 42% and bending stiffness by 54%.  The Ferrari 408 and the Jaguar XJ220 prototypes used this same aluminum technology.<P>One of the two Alcan Fieros went into durability testing and was driven 36,000 miles at the GM Proving Grounds (equivalent to about 100,000 miles elsewhere) with nary a squeak or rattle.  The other was assigned to Pontiac engineer Terry Satchell for further development.  Satchell updated the suspension to 1988 specs and dropped an Olds Quad 4 engine into it.  He later invited members of the press and GM management to drive this car.<P>Everyone came away awed.  The dohc Quad 4, which was easily tweaked to 190 bhp, turned the Fiero into a veritable rocket.  Not only that, but the engine compartment and Getrag 5 speed needed no alterations to accept the Quad 4! It dropped right in with the merest rerouting of hoses and wires....."<P><BR>Man, can you guys imagine that.  Aluminum framed, '88 suspension, 190 Hp Quad 4 and 5 speed??  Why did they cancel the Fiero again??  Who says no corvette competition....<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||12
Z||000025||Wannabe||10-09-2000||11:23 PM||||The Honda S2000 weighs 2809 lbs and very similar overall dimansions to the Fiero. It also has an all aluminum 4 cyl that probably weighs half of the GT's V6. I very much doubt that that Fiero's frame is as rigid as the S2000, but at similar overall size, it ai not overly fat. Motor Trend's complaints about the Fiero's weight were the product of the "sports car prejudice" of the time. The idea was that the Fiero, to be a "sports car" should weigh in like a Triumph Spitfire on an MG. (I didn't hear them complain about the TR-8, at 2900lbs and similar performance to the Fiero GT, or the MR2 Turbo, also at 2900lbs) Let's face it, the Fiero was the favourite whipping boy for the automotive media in the eighties, and they used it as an example of everything wrong with the domestic auto industry, instead of continuing to praise it as a bold and innovative initative, the way they did before it first came out. As "sports cars" go, the Fiero is not a small car, and so is completely repsectable at it 2700 or so lbs. Now, all we need is to find a nice all aluminum turbo 4, dohc V6 or V8 to pop in there, shave a few pounds, add afew ponies....sounds promising to me!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000026||ur2biteme||10-10-2000||08:18 PM||||you're absolutely right about the fiero's treatment in the '80s.  hence the reason for production to be scrapped.  it has taken nearly 10 years for 4 cylinder development to match the super duty powered drag car in '85 (i can't quite remember all the details like driver, owner, etc.) that with only 376 hp on a 3.0L 4cyl cranked out a 9.52 second quarter mile.  i think that the overall car is just fine at 2700 lbs.  <P>ever rolled up next to a miata and actually tried to compare size?  the fiero is a bigger car than it.  be it it's not by much, but all the cars that are lighter are either smaller, more fragile, or both.  <P>they said the fiero was a death trap?  i've got 1 word for you.  Explorer.  at least we have time to get out of our car before we die. in the unlikely event of an engine fire, most cases, you can still coast to the side of the road and get out.  last i remember being in a vehicle of that size (explorer, expedition, excursion, etc) and a tire blew, the damn thing ALMOST rolled with a good driver (it was an 81 dodge ram van).  now, add more ground clearance, higher profile, more prone to blowout tires, and you've got trouble on your hands.  <P>how does all that tie in?  it's the same crap that's been told time and time again about the fiero.  bad press and storys to scare people from buying or owning one.  or at least a pushy salesperson trying to get you to unload yours for something else.  <P>the point i'm making is that the fiero is far from overweight.  it's the negative press that needs to be ignored.  our engines arent that heavy, just underpowered for their size.  with the same drivetrain technology retrofited to these cars (i.e. DOHC, Aluminum engine components, etc.), or even massaging a little more power out of what we've allready got, we would blow the doors off of most of these cars or at least hang with them enough for the competiton to take notice. <P>pardon my bandwidth again   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by ur2biteme (edited 10-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000027||Black88GT||10-10-2000||11:52 PM||||To Motor Trends defense, here is exactly what they printed in the March 1988 edition on the 88 Fiero GT....<P>"The performance envelope is satisfying, but could be improved further by eliminating some of the flab hiding somewhere in the car.  At 2,790 lb, this little GT could stand an austere diet until a couple hundred pounds melted away.  That would enable the greatest advantage from the current 135-hp 2.8-liter V-6 and greatly improved the impact of next years 3.1-liter V-6 of about 150 hp."<P>Also, in general the article was a favorable one.  I don't think you could classify the above statements as "bashing".  It's the truth.  I know a lot of people don't like to hear this, but the Fiero is not a perfect car.  Losing 200 lbs. would benefit greatly.<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
Z||000028||ur2biteme||10-11-2000||03:57 AM||||well, i'm sure it could lose a little, but my point is that it really isn't a heavy car.  from the way i've heard it put elsewhere, the fiero haters want to make it out like this thing weighs in like an LTD.  that and the local pontiac dealer refers to the fiero as the "Pontiac Bar-B-Que".  <P>in my opinion, it could stand to lose a little weight, but my main complaint isn't the total weight, it's this 61/39 balance.  i'd be more satisfied if the car was balanced better as opposed to being lighter.  but i see what you're saying.  trust me, i know the car isn't perfect.  if it were, it'd probably still be produced at a much higher pricetag, and i'd have never gotten mine at the price i did.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>UR2BITEME<BR>'87 econo coupe<BR>170k and a bad fender :(||||reg||1
Z||000029||Old Lar||10-11-2000||03:07 PM||||61/39 balance? Maybe when you remove the engine and do the balance..||||reg||1
Z||000030||gixxer||10-11-2000||07:43 PM||||The F/R balance is more like ~43/57. All race-oriented chassis design books urge you to design in as much rear weight bias as possible for a rear drive car. This assumes that you will size the tires as needed. Obviously, you can put the power down better with this rear weight bias and fat rear tires. <BR>....If you have equal size tires F/R, it is easier to tune the car for neutral handling in steady state cornering if the weight distribution is closer to 50/50. If a Fiero has as much weight as possible moved forward, and wide rear tires, it will tend to understeer more, and probably require a softer front bar, and/or a stiffer rear bar, hurting traction.<BR>...While I'm here, a Fiero doesn't have a true space frame chasis. Neither does it have a "unit" body. It's more of a backbone design with welded outriggers. <BR>With a spaceframe, all the TUBES would either be in tension or compression when loaded. None tubes would be in bending. A passable example is like the triangular trusses used in steel bridge contruction. In fact, with a space frame design, if you were to replace all the welded tube joints with some sort of pivot ball arraingment, the structure wouldn't know the difference and hold its shape.<BR>Since the body panels don't do double duty and add to chassis stiffness, like a unit body design, the Fiero chassis has to be made stronger and stiffer, thus weighing more.<BR>Now that I've stepped on as many toes as possible, I'll go!<BR>Brian    ||||reg||1
Z||000031||count_iblis||10-13-2000||11:20 PM||||so how much lighter is a 3800 sc engine compared to the stock v6?||||reg||1
Z||000032||kevin||10-15-2000||02:14 AM||||Two questions: (1) Who produces an aluminum cradle (weight savings approx. 250 lbs.) (3) Who would have info on aluminum spindles ala Corvette (weight savings approx. 130 lbs.) Maybe some of the newer GM cars would have a substitute part that would bolt up to the Fiero to save weight. Final thought before bed, maybe Alcan of Canada would be interested in offering the aluminum cradle if their was enough interest  from the worldwide FIero owners. Anyway my two thoughts just increased performance by 1.1 seconds in the 1/4 mile and increased milage by 14%! I hope someone out there agrees with me. Anyone...........? night-night ||||reg||1
Z||000033||Formula 18||10-15-2000||05:32 PM||||My friend was over today talk up his Prelude and anyways we got to talking about how much my cars weight and I tell him it is about 2900 with a driver and when we go to look at the sticker on the inside of the door it says my 1988 formula has a Groaa weight od 3140lbs <P>WTF??? <P>can anyone explain this??????||||reg||1
Z||000034||gixxer||10-15-2000||06:14 PM||||"Gross weight" is the design maximum allowed vehicle weight. It is the curb weight(car+ oil+ gas), plus people and cargo. <BR>The curb weight on my A/C equipped Formula is 2780 Lbs.||||reg||1
Z||000035||e_utopia||01-24-2001||04:44 AM||||Sorry to bring an old topic back up, but I just got here and came across this discussion.<P>First, I have to agree strongly that the Fiero is heavier than we'd probably like, but not anywhere near as much heavier as many people would seem to think.  I mean, high power/weight ratio is fun, but it also makes the car harder and harder to drive.  I play with 500 lb cars with 100 HP on the weekends (at least, on weekends during the summer).  It's amazing to drive a car with that kind of ratio, but it you so much as blink at the wrong moment, you better be prepared to do some welding.<P>Second, I plan on getting my first Fiero this summer (almost had one years ago, as my first car but 16-year-old me couldn't find one that was both in decent shape and priced within my budget).  I'm currently in my junior year of college for mechanical engineering, and I'm already involved in building race cars (see abovce paragraph).  So... I'm going to see what sort of stuff I can make out of composites or aluminum, using my car as a guinea pig.<P>Question: does anyone currently offer aluminum cradles or spindles for the Fiero?  If not, I think I'll take the previous poster's suggestion and look there, unless anyone has better ideas.  Maybe I'll have something for the end of the year, or so.<P>--JB<P>------------------<BR>e_utopia@hotmail.com||||reg||1
Z||000036||p4n1c||01-24-2001||08:36 AM||||A friend of the family works at ALCAN, and he's been there for a long while, I wonder if he knows anything about the creation of the Fiero spaceframe there.  It'd be cool to see if he was someone who worked on it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I highly doubt that though.<P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>"Each passing day, every passing face seems like such a blur" <BR>- The Offspring(Million Miles Away)||||reg||10
Z||000037||Phil||01-24-2001||09:02 AM||||My Formula with SC3800 weighs 3068 Lbs including my lard butt(200 lbs) I have air, pw and locks<P>------------------<BR> <BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_1.gif">  <BR>SC 3800||||reg||1
Z||000038||Phil||01-24-2001||09:08 AM||||Thought of another reason that they're "heavy", it's because of all the fun that they have in them and you wouldn't want to take any of that out now would you?||||reg||1
Z||000039||Black88GT||01-24-2001||09:23 AM||||JB, no one makes aluminum spindles/cradle for Fiero's, while I'm not 100% sure of that I think I would have heard something.<P>If someone would make aluminum spindles and an aluminum cradle, that would help a lot with the front rear weight distribution.  I had always wondered about checking into what it would cost to do that.  Please keep us updated.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif"> ||||reg||14
