Z||000042||Will||01-24-2001||05:01 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by kevin:<BR><B>Two questions: (1) Who produces an aluminum cradle (weight savings approx. 250 lbs.) (3) Who would have info on aluminum spindles ala Corvette (weight savings approx. 130 lbs.) Maybe some of the newer GM cars would have a substitute part that would bolt up to the Fiero to save weight. Final thought before bed, maybe Alcan of Canada would be interested in offering the aluminum cradle if their was enough interest  from the worldwide FIero owners. Anyway my two thoughts just increased performance by 1.1 seconds in the 1/4 mile and increased milage by 14%! I hope someone out there agrees with me. Anyone...........? night-night </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Dude, what are you smoking? The steel cradle and cast-iron hub carriers together don't weigh 250 lbs. By going to aluminum cradle and hub carriers you might save 100 lbs. Even fabbed steel hub carriers would save weight over the cast iron ones.||||reg||1
Z||000043||Will||01-24-2001||05:04 PM||||I've been thinking about using a Honda B18C5 powertrain (all aluminum), or B16 head on B20 block, but they are very expensive to get power from, and need redlines of 9K or better to make decent power.<BR>||||reg||1
